      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright 2006 AAGL",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "STUDY OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To compare pain management of standard analgesic and standard analgesic plus diclofenac transdermal patch in patients who undergo laparoscopic gynecologic surgery."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "Randomized prospective study (Canadian Task Force classification I)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "University hospital."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PATIENTS",
            "content": "One hundred twenty patients requiring laparoscopic surgery for gynecologic benign diseases."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
            "content": "Patients were divided into two groups, one medicated with a diclofenac patch (diclofenac group) and the other medicated with standard skin medication (placebo; control group) at all incisional areas at the end of the operation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS",
            "content": "The principal measures of outcome were pain intensity at 6, 12, and 24 hours after surgery and consumption of analgesics. The two treatment groups were comparable with respect to demographic and intraoperative characteristics. No significant difference was observed between the two groups in mean pain intensity at 6 hours after surgery. Mean pain intensity at 12 and 24 hours, respectively, after surgery was significantly lower in the diclofenac group (3.7 +/- 1.3 and 2.0 +/- 0.6) than that observed in the control group (5.7 +/- 1.9 and 4.6 +/- 0.5) (p value, respectively, .002 and <.001). Twenty-one patients (35.0%) in the diclofenac group required analgesics in the first 36 hours after the operation versus 43 patients (71.7%) in the control group (p <.001). Hospital discharge was significantly more rapid in the diclofenac group (28 +/- 5 hours vs 39 +/- 3 hours; p = .031)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Diclofenac transdermal administration seems a valid help to standard analgesic treatment in postoperative pain control and could also help reduce the period of hospitalization of patients who undergo laparoscopic benign gynecologic surgery."
          }
        ]
      },
